# cmd.exe loses its place in a batch file that has LF endings and non-ASCII bytes:
# the lines are then run in fragments. Batch files get CRLF, on every machine.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary files of the module go through Git LFS: git keeps a pointer of a few lines, the
# file itself lies beside it in the LFS store. Without this the history swells - git holds
# a whole copy of a binary on every change and shows no diff.
#
# The cost, stated because it decides what belongs here: LFS does NOT compress one version
# against another. Every edit is stored whole. So this list holds what a recipe cannot
# express and what changes rarely - not the output of every build. Build products of this
# module (morphbench.exe, the launcher's .png preview, the release archive) are named in
# .gitignore and never reach git at all, with or without a rule here.
#
# The list is the formats the workbench itself reads and writes. The .ico is the one that
# exists today: those bytes travel inside morphbench.exe, so the recipe cannot rebuild the
# exe without them. The rest are here as kinds, before the first file of that kind appears,
# because a rule applied only once it hurts is a rule nobody applies - a mesh or a morph
# file carried by tests\ as a fixture would otherwise be committed whole, over and over.
#
# The locale files, dependencies.json and web\ are NOT here on purpose: they are text, and
# they have to diff and merge like any other source.
*.ico   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.nif   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.tri   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.png   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
